[include version_include.man]

[manpage_begin ns_db n [vset version]]
[moddesc {NaviServer Module Commands}]
[titledesc {Database access API}]

[description]
These commands provides a mechanism to access databases.


[section {COMMANDS}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd "ns_db 1row"] \
    [arg handle] \
    [opt [option -bindvars]] \
    [opt "[option -bindset] [arg setId]"] \
    [opt "[option -binddict] [arg dictionary]"] \
    [opt "[option -bindtypes] [arg types]"] \
    [opt [option -emptynull]] \
    [opt [option --]] \
    [arg sql]]

This command expects the SQL to be a select statement that returns exactly one
row and returns that row as an ns_set. An error is returned if zero or more than
one row is returned.


[call [cmd "ns_db 0or1row"] \
    [arg handle] \
    [opt [option -bindvars]] \
    [opt "[option -bindset] [arg setId]"] \
    [opt "[option -binddict] [arg dictionary]"] \
    [opt "[option -bindtypes] [arg types]"] \
    [opt [option -emptynull]] \
    [opt [option --]] \
    [arg sql]]

This command expects the provided SQL command
to be a select statement that returns exactly zero
or one row. On zero rows, a null string is returned. On one row, a newly
allocated ns_set is returned. An error is thrown if more than one row
is returned.


[call [cmd "ns_db batchinfo"] [opt "[option -handle] [arg value]"] [opt [arg batch]]]

Without [option -handle], [arg batch] is required. With [option -handle],
[arg value] is a checked-out database handle and [arg batch] is optional.

Return immutable-batch metadata as a Tcl dictionary without materializing record
values. Batches are opaque, interpreter-local values produced by registered C
producers such as [cmd "ns_accesslog scan"]. Metadata includes the shared schema,
record count and producer-specific source/checkpoint information. Modifying the
returned Tcl dictionary does not mutate the batch. A diagnostic string label is
not a serializable or reconstructible batch handle.

[para]
With [option -handle], report the checked-out handle's batch capabilities. supported is false for a
driver without registered batch callbacks. Registered drivers report their method,
base types, supported refinements/representations and transaction requirements.
The tclRows boolean reports support for prepared Tcl-row input on this handle.
Older cores omit this key.
With a batch, compatible reports schema/type compatibility; it does not validate
individual values, a destination table's affinities or its constraints.

[call [cmd "ns_db batchinsert"] \
    [arg handle] \
    [option "-table [arg table]"] \
    [opt "[option -columns] [arg mapping]"] \
    [opt "[option -schema] [arg schema]"] \
    [opt "[option -nulls] [arg nulls]"] \
    [arg batchOrRows] \
    ]

Insert synchronously through a driver's explicitly registered batch implementation
using the existing checked-out connection and an active caller-owned transaction.
A driver without callbacks raises NS_DB BATCH UNSUPPORTED before database work;
there is no automatic SQL-text, JSON or other insertion fallback.

[para] table is a Tcl list of one or two identifier components, each quoted by the
backend. It is not an SQL fragment. mapping is a dictionary of source-field names
to destination-column names. Omitted mapping uses all schema names unchanged.
Omitted destination columns use database defaults. Empty mappings, unknown source
fields, duplicate target names and unsupported types/refinements are rejected.
Targets must already exist; there is no table creation, conflict policy, upsert,
deduplication or automatic record transformation.

[para] The batch remains unchanged and retained by its caller. Native sinks read
borrowed lexical byte slices against the shared schema, with explicit nulls and
conversion at the sink. No per-record Tcl dictionaries, triples or serialization
are required. The initial shared provider ABI supports flat string, number,
boolean and null columns; nested object/array schemas are explicitly rejected.
JSON base types and optional schema refinements remain separate.

[para] Prepared Tcl rows can be inserted directly with:

[example_begin]
 ns_db dml $handle {BEGIN IMMEDIATE}
 
 ns_db batchinsert $handle -table events -schema {
     id event-rows version 1 columns {
         {name id type number refinement int64 representation integer_decimal}
         {name label type string nullable true}
     }
 } -nulls {{} {1}} \
 {{1 {a label}} {2 {}}}
 
 ns_db dml $handle COMMIT
[example_end]

[para] With [option -schema], the final argument is a list of rows rather than
an opaque C batch. Each row is a positional Tcl list with exactly one value per
schema column. The schema uses the same flat descriptors and lexical values
as C batches (for example, true/false for booleans and hexadecimal strings for
encoded binary).
[option -columns] retains its source-name to destination-name mapping semantics.
Optional [option -nulls] is a parallel list containing zero-based NULL column
indices for each row; it requires [option -schema]. Omission means no explicit
NULL values except columns of base type null. Empty strings remain values and
are never interpreted as NULL. Explicit NULLs require nullable columns.

[para] All row widths and NULL indices are checked before insertion. Typed value
conversion and destination compatibility remain with the driver. The input rows
are retained synchronously and are not modified. No application C producer,
opaque batch object or separate conversion command is required. The core borrows
ASCII lexical values and converts non-ASCII Tcl strings to external UTF-8 when
needed. Rows are not SQL fragments. Existing opaque C batch input remains
unchanged when [option -schema] is omitted.

[para] Success reports a dictionary with rows and method. The count represents
changes within the transaction, not a durable commit. The driver must finish all
native readers/statements before returning. An empty batch still requires a
supported driver, valid mapping/target and caller transaction, then reports zero
rows. A disconnected handle, active result or conflicting prepared operation is
rejected without discarding its result.

[para] The command never commits, rolls back or consumes the batch. An error can
leave earlier rows written in the outer transaction: the caller must roll back.
Errors use NS_DB BATCH codes such as TYPE_MISMATCH, MAPPING,
TRANSACTION_REQUIRED, CONVERSION or INSERT; native driver diagnostics can append
zero-based row/field information and populate the ordinary nsdb exception. An
application can atomically insert records, update rollups and save a producer
checkpoint before committing the same transaction. Checkpoint serialization must
preserve binary fields, for example by base64-encoding a UTF-8 Tcl-list envelope
when storing the checkpoint as database text.

[para] The SQLite implementation uses reusable prepared bindings on the same
connection and checks its actual transaction state, including outer SAVEPOINTs.
It enforces native int32/int64 ranges and exact integer-microsecond conversions
without doubles. Access-log calendar/epoch timestamp strings can become UTC
microseconds in INTEGER/NUMERIC targets, or remain lexical in TEXT targets.
Generic JSON numbers can remain exact lexemes in explicit TEXT storage.
Encoded-binary hex refinements map to BLOB. Unknown refinements fail explicitly.

[para] SQLite target affinities are checked before row insertion to prevent silent
numeric coercion or integer-to-REAL precision loss. Ordinary strings require
TEXT or no-affinity/BLOB storage, native integers/booleans/microseconds require
INTEGER/NUMERIC or no-affinity/BLOB storage, and encoded binary requires BLOB.
Generated/hidden columns are rejected. Database constraints and triggers retain
their normal semantics. SQLite rows counts direct changes, excluding trigger
side effects, matching SQLite changes semantics. Exact conversion can reject a
source spelling or value even when its base type is supported.

[call [cmd "ns_db bindinfo"] [arg handle] [opt [option "-version|-types"]]]

Reports whether the driver supports
bind vars in SQL statements. Its optional [option -version] returns Tcl binding API version
6 for native one-dimensional array metadata (5 denotes binary values and row
column types; 4 denotes untyped rows;
3 denotes scalar/list types; 2 denotes the preceding
scalar-only options), or 0 when unsupported. The default
boolean result remains compatible with the earlier binding API.

[para] With [option -types], returns the supported native parameter
interpretations: [const scalar], [const binary], and optionally [const array].
Core list/row expansions remain independent of native array capabilities.

[call [cmd "ns_db bindrow"] [arg handle]]

Returns an ns_set structure whose key names are the column names
of the rows returned by the SQL command previously-executed with ns_db exec.
If the database is not currently returning rows (i.e., a status other than
NS_ROWS was returned by ns_db exec), an error is thrown. The handle argument is
a database handle (obtained with ns_db gethandle).


[call [cmd "ns_db bouncepool"] [arg poolname]]

Marks all database handles for the specified database pool as stale. When any
database handle currently open is put back into the pool, its connection to the
database will be reset.


[call [cmd "ns_db cancel"] [arg handle]]

Cancel the current operation.


[call [cmd "ns_db connected"] [arg handle]]

Returns a boolean value indicating whether the connection to the database pool is made.

[call [cmd "ns_db currenthandles"]]

Returns information about handles currently allocated to the thread
performing this call. The result contains a dict with the pools,
from which handles were allocated, the names of the handles and their
"active" state (a handle is active between a "ns_db select" and the
last "ns_db getrow" statement). If no handles are allocated by this
thread, empty is returned.

[example_begin]
 # [lb]ns_db currenthandles[rb]
 main {nsdb0 1} subquery {nsdb1 0}
[example_end]

in the example above the result indicates
that the current thread has allocated handles from pool
[term main] and [term subquery], but the handle from
pool [term main] (i.e. [term nsdb0])
is currently active (e.g., during a [term select]).


[call [cmd "ns_db datasource"] [arg handle]]

Returns the datasource for the database pool.


[call [cmd "ns_db dbtype"] [arg handle]]

Returns the database type for the database pool.


[call [cmd "ns_db dml"] \
    [arg handle] \
    [opt [option -bindvars]] \
    [opt "[option -bindset] [arg setId]"] \
    [opt "[option -binddict] [arg dictionary]"] \
    [opt "[option -bindtypes] [arg types]"] \
    [opt [option -emptynull]] \
    [opt [option --]] \
    [arg sql]]

Executes the provided SQL DML statement that should be data
manipulation language such as an insert or update, or data definition
language such as a create table.


[call [cmd "ns_db driver"] [arg handle]]

Returns the name of the driver of the handle.


[call [cmd "ns_db exception"] [arg handle]]

Returns the most recent exception for the database pool.


[call [cmd "ns_db exec"] \
    [arg handle] \
    [opt [option -bindvars]] \
    [opt "[option -bindset] [arg setId]"] \
    [opt "[option -binddict] [arg dictionary]"] \
    [opt "[option -bindtypes] [arg types]"] \
    [opt [option -emptynull]] \
    [opt [option --]] \
    [arg sql]]

Executes the specified SQL command. It returns either NS_DML (if the SQL command
is a DML or DDL command) or NS_ROWS (if the SQL command returns rows, such as
a SELECT). This function can be used for ad hoc querying, where you don't know
what kind of SQL command will be executed.


[call [cmd "ns_db flush"] [arg handle]]

Flushes the results of an SQL select so you do not need to use ns_db
getrow to get all the rows and throw them away.


[call [cmd "ns_db gethandle"] \
     [opt [option "-timeout [arg time]"]] \
     [opt --] \
     [opt [arg poolname]] \
     [opt [arg nhandles]] ]

Returns the specified number of handles from the specified pool.
If poolname is not specified, the Defaultpool from the configuration file is
used. If nhandles is not specified, 1 handle is returned. (Note that if you
specify nhandles, you must also specify a poolname.) If not enough handles are
available to fulfill the request, it waits until they are available. You must
request all the handles you will need for a specific pool with one call to ns_db
gethandle. You must release all your database handles explicitly (with ns_db
releasehandle) before acquiring more. If you request multiple handles from the
database, this function returns a Tcl list of database handles (space
delimited). In this case, each handle must be released with a separate call to
ns_db releasehandle.

[para]
If [option -timeout] is not specified or timeout is zero, [cmd "ns_db gethandle"] will
wait indefinitely (perhaps forever) for the requested number of handles to
become available. If timeout is greater than zero, ns_db gethandle will either
return with the handles within that time period, or return "" if the time period
was exceeded, or generate a Tcl error.
See the examples below.
The argument [arg time] can be specified in the form
[arg secs[opt :microsecs]], or [arg secs.fraction],
or as a number with the time units ms, s, m, d.


These are valid uses of ns_db gethandle:

[example_begin]
 ns_db gethandle
 # 1 handle from default pool
 
 ns_db gethandle -timeout 2.5s
 # 1 handle from default pool, 2.5 sec timeout
 
 ns_db gethandle -timeout -1 poolname
 # 1 handle from poolname, error if not available
 
 ns_db gethandle poolname
 # 1 handle from poolname
 
 ns_db gethandle -timeout 23 poolname
 # 1 handle from poolname, 23 sec timeout
 
 ns_db gethandle poolname 5
 # 5 handles from poolname
 
 ns_db gethandle -timeout 2.0s poolname 5
 # 5 handles from poolname, 2.0 sec timeout
[example_end]

[call [cmd "ns_db getrow"] [arg handle] [arg setId]]

Fetches the next row from the result of a prior
[cmd "ns_db select"] call, inserting its values into the [cmd ns_set]
identified by [arg setId] (the same set returned by the [cmd "ns_db
select"]). 
The command returns "1" if there are more rows waiting
and returns "0" otherwise.
Calling [cmd "ns_db getrow"] again after receiving `0` will raise an error.


[call [cmd "ns_db info"] [arg handle]]

Returns a Tcl dictionary with metadata about the database connection
associated with [arg handle].  The dictionary may include:

[const clientversion] (version of the client library),
[const serverversion] (version of the connected database server)
[const type] (the database type (e.g., [const PostgreSQL], [const Oracle])
[const pool] (the connection pool name from which this handle was allocated).

Note: not all drivers supply every field; for instance, some may omit version numbers.


[call [cmd "ns_db logminduration"] [opt [arg poolname]] [opt [arg minduration]]]

 Queries or sets the minimum execution time threshold for SQL
 statement logging. When SQL debug logging is enabled (for example via
 "ns_logctl severity Debug(sql) ..."), only statements that take at
 least the specified duration will be emitted to the system log.

If [arg poolname] is omitted, the operation is applied on all database
pools. The [arg minduration] can be specified in
the form [arg secs[opt :microsecs]], or [arg secs.fraction], or as a
number with the time units ms, s, m, d.


Change the verbosity level to show all SQL commands or only these taking
longer:
[example_begin]
 #
 # Increase verbosity of SQL
 #
 ns_logctl debug(sql) on
 
 #
 # Set for every pool the longminduration to 0 to show all
 # SQL commands (or e.g. to 10ms, meaning: show SQL commands
 # taking longer than 10 milliseconds).
 #
 foreach pool [lb]ns_db pools[rb] {ns_db logminduration $pool 0}
[example_end]

[call [cmd "ns_db password"] [arg handle]]

Returns the password of the user for the database pool.


[call [cmd "ns_db poolname"] [arg handle]]

Returns the database pool that this handle came from.


[call [cmd "ns_db pools"]]

Returns a list of all database pools.


[call [cmd "ns_db releasehandle"] [arg handle]]

Puts the handle back in the pool. The server will automatically return any open
handles to the pool after a page has finished executing.


[call [cmd "ns_db rowcount"] [arg handle]]

Returns number of processed records by the last SQL statements,
this is useful with INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE
statements to know how many records updated.


[call [cmd "ns_db select"] \
    [arg handle] \
    [opt [option -bindvars]] \
    [opt "[option -bindset] [arg setId]"] \
    [opt "[option -binddict] [arg dictionary]"] \
    [opt "[option -bindtypes] [arg types]"] \
    [opt [option -emptynull]] \
    [opt [option --]] \
    [arg sql]]

Executes the SQL statement on the database server. It returns an
ns_set with the keys set to the column names that were selected. Use ns_db
getrow to retrieve rows. You cannot perform nested select statements. Before you
start a new select statement, you must first either retrieve all the rows from
the first select or use the ns_db flush statement to flush any rows not
yet retrieved.


[call [cmd "ns_db setexception"] [arg handle] [arg code] [arg message]]

Returns the specified status code and message to the client.


[call [cmd "ns_db sp_exec"] [arg handle]]

Executes a stored procedure that has been initialized with
[cmd "ns_db sp_start"] and [cmd "ns_db sp_setparam"]. It returns "NS_DML" if the command was
successfully executed but did not return rows, or it returns "NS_ROWS" if the
command was successfully executed and did return rows (which can then be fetched
with [cmd "ns_db bindrow"] and [cmd "ns_db getrow"]). It throws an error if the command failed.
This function is implemented only for the Sybase database driver. See [cmd "ns_db sp_start"] for an example.


[call [cmd "ns_db sp_getparams"] [arg handle]]
]
Returns any output parameters set after executing a stored
procedure with [cmd "ns_db sp_exec"]. It returns an ns_set or throws an error on failure.

[call [cmd "ns_db sp_returncode"] [arg handle]]

ns_db sp_returncode gets the return code from a stored procedure. It must be
called after ns_db sp_exec. This function is implemented only for the Sybase
database driver. See [cmd "ns_db sp_start"] for an example.


[call [cmd "ns_db sp_setparam"] \
     [arg handle] \
     [arg paramname] \
     [arg type] \
     [const in|out] \
     [arg value] ]

Sets a parameter for a call to a stored
procedure. [arg paramname] is the name of the parameter, for example
[term @name].  [arg type] is the data type of this parameter, for
example [term varchar]. The nextg argument denotes the direction, whether it
is an input or output parameter. It must be set to either [term in] or
[term out].  [arg value] denotes the parameter value to send. This function
returns 1 on success and throws an error on failure. This function is
implemented only for the Sybase database driver. See [cmd "ns_db sp_start"] for an example.


[call [cmd "ns_db sp_start"] [arg handle] [arg procname]]

Begins execution of the stored procedure called [arg procname].
It returns 0 on success and throws an error on failure. This function is
implemented only for the Sybase database driver. See the example below.


[example_begin]
 set db [lb]ns_db gethandle $pool[rb]
 set ret [lb]ns_db sp_start $db "p_TestProc"[rb]
 
 #
 # Set the parameters for this stored procedure.  The SQL definition of this
 # procedure is:
 #
 #   CREATE PROCEDURE p_TestProc(@x int, @y varchar(16) out, @z int out)
 #
 # The arguments to ns_db sp_setparam are like this:
 #
 #   ns_db sp_setparam $handle $paramname $type in|out $value
 #
 set ret [lb]ns_db sp_setparam $db "@x" int in 4[rb]
 set ret [lb]ns_db sp_setparam $db "@y" varchar out "varchar val"[rb]
 set ret [lb]ns_db sp_setparam $db "@z" int out 231[rb]
 
 #
 # Execute the stored procedure now
 #
 set ret [lb]ns_db sp_exec $db[rb]
[example_end]

[call [cmd "ns_db stats"] [opt "[arg handle] [opt [option {-database name}]]"]]

Without [arg handle], returns a Tcl dictionary of statistics for all pools.
The statistics contain the number of SQL statements executed, the number of
get-handle operations, the number of currently connected database connections,
the total and used handles, and the aggregated wait time for handles from each
pool (including connection setup time to the database server).

[para]
With [arg handle], returns statistics from the optional driver callback for
that owned database handle. The result is a Tcl dictionary with [const driver]
(the driver's name) and [const supported] (a boolean). Without a callback,
[const supported] is false; existing drivers require no modification.
The [option -database] option requires [arg handle].

[para]
Statistics use a shared vocabulary where the scopes and units are comparable:

[list_begin itemized]
 [item] [const connection]: 
   [const memoryUsedBytes] and [const cacheUsedBytes] are current memory gauges
   in bytes. [const cacheHits] and [const cacheMisses] are cumulative cache-access
   counts for this connection. Backend documentation defines the cache
   coverage.
 
 [item] [const statement]: 
   [const executions] counts execution starts of the currently retained statement;
   [const rowsReturned] counts rows delivered to the caller by that statement.
   [const memoryUsedBytes] is its current attributed memory in bytes. These are
   statement-instance metrics, not historical connection or server
   totals.
   
 [item] [const highwater]: 
   Meaningful peak gauges, nested at the same paths as their corresponding current
   metrics. Omit peaks that the engine does not measure. Cumulative counters are
   not peaks, even if an engine API returns them in an output called
   highwater.
   
 [item] [const backend]: 
   Driver-specific fields, nested under the driver's identifier. SQLite places
   its connection/statement details, pool policy and WAL snapshot under
   [const {backend sqlite}]. These are not equated with PostgreSQL server-wide
   buffer, scan or checkpoint statistics.
[list_end]

[para]
Metric values are scalars, without per-metric [const available], [const current]
or [const highwater] wrappers. Omit unsupported or unavailable metrics; zero
means a measured zero. Omit empty groups. [const supported] reports callback
support only, not the availability of every metric. A backend may retain a
reason/code when a supported measurement temporarily fails, without providing
unavailable numerical values. SQLite, for example, omits WAL frame counts when
its snapshot is locked and reports the reason under [const {backend sqlite wal}].

[para]
An explicit [option -database] selects a database within the connection where
supported; SQLite defaults to [const main] and accepts attached database names.
Unknown databases and unsupported explicit selection are errors. Connection
counters still cover the connection; the selector chooses the SQLite WAL scope.
[cmd {ns_sqlite stats}] retains its legacy names, availability wrappers and
sentinels for existing applications; it is a compatibility view of the same
sampler rather than the shared result format.

[para]
A supported callback requires a connected handle. Statistics do not reconnect
it, flush or consume pending rows, reset counters, commit or roll back work,
or clear database error diagnostics. Callback errors propagate to the caller.
Different drivers' counters need not be comparable; this command does not issue
queries against PostgreSQL server statistics views.


[para]
Driver authors can implement the optional [const DbFn_Stats] callback,
declared by [const NS_DB_STATS_ABI] in [file nsdb.h]. Existing callback
IDs and the public handle layout are unchanged.

[example_begin]
typedef int (Ns_DbStatsProc)(Tcl_Interp *interp, Ns_DbHandle *handle,
                           const char *database);
[example_end]

The callback returns TCL_OK with a dictionary as the interpreter result, or
TCL_ERROR with its diagnostic. A NULL database selects the driver's default
scope; an explicit name must be validated or rejected. The callback borrows the
handle and database name only for the call. Core adds [const driver] and
[const supported] to the result, and validates that successful results are
dictionaries. [const Ns_DbStats] is the corresponding C dispatch function.

[para]
Statistics callbacks are observational: preserve pending results, transactions,
affected-row counts and database error diagnostics; do not reconnect, reset
counters or run maintenance. Document scopes, units, unavailable counters and
sampling costs per driver.



[call [cmd "ns_db user"] [arg handle]]

Returns the user (as specified for the User parameter of the configuration file)
for the database pool.


[call [cmd "ns_db withhandle"] \
    [opt "[option -pool] [arg value]"] \
    [opt "[option -handle] [arg value]"] \
    [opt "[option -timeout] [arg value]"] \
    [opt [option --]] \
    [arg handleVar] [arg script]]

Evaluate [arg script] in the caller's frame with a database handle temporarily
assigned to [arg handleVar]. The variable may be a scalar or an array element.
Its previous value is restored afterward; a previously absent variable is
removed. The body's result and Tcl completion options, including errors,
[cmd return], [cmd break], [cmd continue] and custom completion codes, are
preserved. A variable-restoration error is reported if the body succeeded;
otherwise the body's original completion takes precedence. Variable traces
can prevent assignment or restoration and should not reject these operations.

[para]
Without [option -handle], acquire one handle from the pool specified by
[option -pool], or from the
configured default pool when [option -pool] is omitted. Release this owned
handle on every exit, including assignment failure and script errors.
[option -timeout] has the same units and waiting semantics as
[cmd "ns_db gethandle"], including indefinite waiting when omitted or zero.
Unlike [cmd "ns_db gethandle"], a checkout timeout raises an error with
[const {NS DB TIMEOUT}] as its error code and does not execute the body.
Pool permissions and the prohibition on acquiring more handles from an
already-owned pool remain unchanged.

[para]
With [option -handle], borrow an existing handle owned by this interpreter.
The borrowed handle is not released, flushed or reset on leaving the scope.
This form can be nested and used within a caller-managed transaction. [option -handle] cannot be
combined with [option -pool] or [option -timeout]. There is no implicit reuse
of other checked-out handles.

[para]
This command does not begin, commit or roll back a transaction itself.
An owned handle follows the normal [cmd "ns_db releasehandle"] cleanup path,
including driver flush/reset. Finish transactions and consume results before
leaving an owning scope; do not return its handle for later use or manually
release it inside the body. Keep unrelated work outside the scope to avoid
holding a connection unnecessarily. Borrowed handles retain their existing
transaction and result-set state except for changes explicitly made by the body.

[example_begin]
 ns_db withhandle -pool records -timeout 100ms db {
     ns_db dml $db -bindvars {
         UPDATE records SET value = :value WHERE id = :id
     }
 }

 # Use a caller-owned connection without returning it to the pool.
 ns_db withhandle -handle $transactionHandle db {
     update_records $db
 }
[example_end]


[call [cmd "ns_dberrorcode"] [arg handle]]

Returns the database error code for the specified database handle.

[call [cmd "ns_dberrormsg"] [arg handle]]

Returns the database error message for the specified database handle.

[call [cmd "ns_dbconfigpath"]]

Returns the database section name file for the nsdb module from the
configuration file.

[call [cmd "ns_dbpooldescription"] [arg poolname]]

Returns the description of the provided database pool name from the
[lb]ns/db/pools[rb] section in the configuration file.

[list_end]


[section {COMMON BIND OPTIONS}]

The commands [cmd {ns_db exec}], [cmd {ns_db dml}], [cmd {ns_db select}],
[cmd {ns_db 1row}] and [cmd {ns_db 0or1row}] accept these options after
[arg handle] and before [arg sql]. Without binding options, their existing
SQL execution behavior is unchanged. Binding requires a supporting driver;
use [cmd {ns_db bindinfo}] to check support.

[list_begin definitions]
[def [option -bindvars]]
Resolve colon-prefixed SQL parameters from Tcl variables in the caller's scope.
For example, [const :id] reads the variable [const id].

[def "[option -bindset] [arg setId]"]
Resolve parameters from the ns_set identified by [arg setId]. Keys are parameter
names without the colon prefix.

[def "[option -binddict] [arg dictionary]"]
Resolve parameters from the Tcl [arg dictionary]. Keys are parameter names
without the colon prefix.

[def "[option -bindtypes] [arg types]"]
Use the Tcl dictionary [arg types] to specify parameter interpretations.
Keys are parameter names without the colon prefix; values specify
[const scalar], [const binary], [const list], [const rows] or [const array],
with optional metadata as described in the bind variables section below.
Parameters not listed use [const scalar]. This option requires a binding source.

[def [option -emptynull]]
Map explicitly supplied empty scalar values and empty collection elements to
SQL NULL. Without this option, empty text remains empty text.
Empty native arrays remain empty arrays; empty list and row containers are
errors. Explicit binary values are also mapped to NULL when empty.
This option requires a binding source and does not permit missing bindings.

[def [option --]]
End option processing, for example when the SQL argument starts with a hyphen.
[list_end]

Exactly one of [option -bindvars], [option -bindset] or [option -binddict]
selects the binding source. These sources are mutually exclusive. Missing
bindings raise errors; explicit sources never fall back to caller variables.


[section {BIND VARIABLES}]

Bind variables are named placeholders for values in an SQL statement. The SQL
contains names such as [const :id], where the [const :] denotes the
begin of a bind variable and [const id] is its name. The application
supplies their values for the used names separately through Tcl
variables, an ns_set or a dictionary.

[para]
Use binding instead of interpolating or quoting application values into SQL.
A bound value is data, even when it contains quotes or SQL syntax. This prevents
SQL injection through those values, reduces quoting mistakes and avoids repeated
SQL string construction. Table names, column names and SQL fragments cannot be
bound: keep these fixed or validate them separately. Binding does not make
application-generated SQL fragments safe.


[subsection {Scalar values}]

The simplest form uses [option -bindvars] to read Tcl variables from the caller's
scope. Brace the SQL so Tcl leaves its text unchanged:

[example_begin]
 set id 42
 set row [lb]ns_db 1row $handle -bindvars {
     SELECT label FROM items WHERE id = :id
 }[rb]
 set label [lb]ns_set get $row label[rb]
 ns_set free $row
[example_end]

The same source works for writes. Quotes in [const label] require no application
escaping:

[example_begin]
 set label "John's item"
 ns_db dml $handle -bindvars {
     UPDATE items SET label = :label WHERE id = :id
 }
[example_end]

[para]
Use [option -binddict] when values already reside in a dictionary. Its keys omit
the colon prefix; missing keys are errors rather than references to Tcl variables:

[example_begin]
 set values [lb]dict create id 42 label "John's item"[rb]
 ns_db dml $handle -binddict $values {
     UPDATE items SET label = :label WHERE id = :id
 }
[example_end]

[para]
For an ns_set, use [option -bindset] instead:

[example_begin]
 set values [lb]ns_set create bindings id 42 label "John's item"[rb]
 ns_db dml $handle -bindset $values {
     UPDATE items SET label = :label WHERE id = :id
 }
 ns_set free $values
[example_end]

[para]
Scalar is the default interpretation, including for numeric Tcl objects and
values whose internal representation happens to be a list or bytearray. The
database applies its normal column conversions. Use [option -bindtypes] only
when a different interpretation of the supplied values is required.

[subsection {Lists and rows}]

A [const list] expands a Tcl list into separate parameters. SQL supplies the
surrounding parentheses, as in an IN clause:

[example_begin]
 set ids {42 57 91}
 set status active
 set rows [lb]ns_db select $handle -bindvars -bindtypes {ids list} {
     SELECT id FROM items WHERE id IN (:ids) AND status = :status
 }[rb]
 while {[lb]ns_db getrow $handle $rows[rb]} {
     # Consume each row using ns_set.
 }
 ns_db flush $handle
 ns_set free $rows
[example_end]

A [const rows] value expands equal-width Tcl row lists into parenthesized rows
of parameters. Do not add parentheses around its placeholder:

[example_begin]
 set values {{42 first} {57 second}}
 ns_db dml $handle -bindvars -bindtypes {values rows} {
     INSERT INTO items (id, label) VALUES :values
 }
[example_end]

Lists and rows must be nonempty and well formed. Rows must have a nonzero,
consistent width. Values remain parameters; no element is concatenated into
SQL. Row cells use scalar semantics unless column types specify binary values.

[subsection {Binary values}]

[const binary] uses Tcl bytearray semantics, without decoding hex or base64.
It supports embedded NUL and high bytes; SQLite stores these values as BLOBs:

[example_begin]
 set payload [lb]binary format H* 00ff0102[rb]
 ns_db dml $handle -bindvars -bindtypes {payload binary} {
     INSERT INTO payloads (data) VALUES (:payload)
 }
[example_end]

For rows, specify one type per column. Only [const scalar] and [const binary]
column types are supported, and the type list must match the row width:

[example_begin]
 set key [lb]binary format H* 00ff[rb]
 set values [lb]list [lb]list $key 192.0.2.1 1700000000[rb][rb]
 ns_db dml $handle -bindvars \
     -bindtypes {values {rows {binary scalar scalar}}} {
         INSERT INTO peers (k, address, seen) VALUES :values
     }
[example_end]

Drivers without binary support reject explicit binary values instead of
silently converting them to text.

[subsection {Native SQL arrays}]

[const array] binds a Tcl list as one native one-dimensional SQL array parameter.
Unlike [const list], it does not expand into separate SQL parameters. PostgreSQL
supports this form; unsupported drivers reject it explicitly. A SQL cast or the
target column determines the element type:

[example_begin]
 set ids {42 57 91}
 set rows [lb]ns_db select $handle -bindvars -bindtypes {ids array} {
     SELECT id FROM items WHERE id = ANY(:ids::integer[lb][rb])
 }[rb]
 while {[lb]ns_db getrow $handle $rows[rb]} {
     # Consume each row using ns_set.
 }
 ns_db flush $handle
 ns_set free $rows
[example_end]

Empty arrays are valid. Nested Tcl lists are text elements, not subarrays.
Optional [const {array delimiter}] metadata selects a printable, non-reserved
ASCII delimiter; the default is a comma. Literal text such as NULL never acts
as a special sentinel.

[subsection {Empty values and SQL NULL}]

By default, an empty scalar is empty text and an empty binary value is a
zero-length binary value. SQL NULL is different from both. Use
[option -emptynull] when explicitly supplied empty values should mean SQL NULL:

[example_begin]
 set id 42
 set label {}
 ns_db dml $handle -bindvars -emptynull {
     UPDATE items SET label = :label WHERE id = :id
 }
[example_end]

This stores SQL NULL in [const label]. Without [option -emptynull], it stores
empty text. The option applies to all supplied empty scalar and binary values,
list elements, row cells and native array elements in the call. It preserves an
empty native array as an empty array; it does not make empty list or row
containers valid. There is no magic string representing NULL. Missing bindings
always raise errors, even with [option -emptynull].

[subsection {Execution and parameter names}]

Each command retains its usual result and transaction behavior. Binding neither
commits nor rolls back. One complete SQL statement is executed; additional SQL
after it is rejected. Unsupported drivers raise errors when binding is requested.
[cmd {ns_db bindinfo}] checks support, and its [option -types] form reports native
parameter types. Malformed types, unused type metadata and missing bindings are
errors before execution. SQL logging records the template rather than bound
values. Text values and SQL containing NUL are rejected; use binary binding for
binary data.

[para]
Parameter names follow [cmd ns_pg_bind]: the first byte must be a letter
(C [const isalpha]) or underscore; subsequent bytes may be alphanumeric
(C [const isalnum]), underscore, dollar sign or number sign. Classification uses
the current C locale, not Unicode character properties. For example,
[const :phone#] and [const {:oracle$number}] each identify one parameter.
Dollar signs continue names; use colon-prefixed names in application SQL.

[para]
SQL literals, quoted identifiers, doubled quote delimiters, SQLite bracket and
backtick identifiers, and line/block comments do not introduce parameters.
PostgreSQL casts are skipped. PostgreSQL escape-string and dollar-quoted bodies
are not implemented by this lexer.

[subsection {Caching and driver integration}]

The SQL Tcl object caches parsed parameter names and externally encoded SQL.
Collection expansion caches one SQL shape; changing collection lengths rebuilds
that shape without reparsing the original SQL. Every call resolves current
values. This is not a connection-level prepared-statement cache.

[para]
Expanded parameters use driver markers that cannot collide with application
names. Changes to SQL text, the expansion shape, marker factory or system
encoding invalidate the corresponding cached representation. Shared plans and
encoded buffers remain retained during nested driver calls.

[para]
Driver authors can implement [const DbFn_Bind] for scalar text or
[const DbFn_BindTyped] for typed parameters. The typed callback is preferred;
a NULL types array means all text, and registering only the typed callback is
sufficient. Native arrays additionally require the corresponding driver support.

[section CONFIGURATION]

The [term nsdb] module is typically loaded per-server, where every server
might use multiple database pools. Below is a sample configuration for
PostgreSQL using the [term nsdbpg] driver, using two database pools
where [term pool1] is the default pool.

[example_begin]
 ns_section "ns/db/drivers" {
   ns_param	postgres        ${bindir}/nsdbpg.so
 }
 
 ns_section ns/server/${server}/modules {
   ns_param	nsdb		${bindir}/nsdb.so
 }
 
 ns_section ns/server/${server}/db {
   ns_param	pools           pool1,pool2
   ns_param	defaultpool     pool1
 }
 
 ns_section ns/db/pools {
   ns_param	pool1           "Pool 1"
   ns_param	pool2           "Pool 2"
 }
 
 ns_section ns/db/pool/pool1 {
   ns_param	...
 }
[example_end]

For every pool the following configuration options might be used:

[list_begin definitions]

[def CheckInterval] Check after this time interval for
stale connections. Default: 5m.

[def Connections] Integer: The maximum number of connections that can
be established at any one time. The server automatically makes
connections as needed up to this maximum number. If additional
connections are requested during processing, the requests must wait.
Default: 2

[def Datasource] Name specific to the database management system that
might be necessary for opening database connections. For PostgreSQL,
datasource has the form "${db_host}:${db_port}:${db_name}". See also:
[uri https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/libpq-connect.html#LIBPQ-PARAMKEYWORDS]

[def Driver] Name of the driver for this pool as specified in
[term ns/db/drivers].

[def LogMinDuration] Time interval for logging SQL statements. When SQL
logging is turned on, log only SQL queries that take longer than the
specified duration (similar to log_min_duration_statement in
PostgreSQL). Setting [term LogMinDuration] to 0 causes all SQL
statements to be logged.

[def LogSQLErrors] Boolean value: If set to [term On], SQL errors are
written to the server log along with the offending SQL statement(s).

[def MaxIdle] The maximum time interval that a database
connection within this pool can remain open and idle.  The default
setting of "10m" causes connections that are idle for 10 minutes to be
closed. Note that [term MaxIdle] will not have an effect if it is
equal to [term MaxOpen]. Setting [term MaxIdle] to 0 deactivates the
automatic closing feature on idle connections.
Default: 5m

[def MaxOpen] The maximum time interval that a database
connection within this pool can remain open.  The default setting of
"60m" causes connections to keep open for one hour regardless of
activity level. Database connections will then be opened again as
needed up to the value of the [term Connections] parameter.  It is
recommended that [term MaxOpen] be a multiple of [term MaxIdle];
otherwise, [term MaxOpen] may be off by as much as (MaxOpen mod MaxIdle).
Setting [term MaxOpen] to 0 deactivates the automatic closing feature.
Default: 60m

[def Password] Password to log in to the database.

[def User] Username to log in to the database.

[list_end]

All time intervals can be specified with and without a time unit
suffix. Valid time units are "ms", "s", "m", "h", "d".
If no time unit suffix is specified, seconds are assumed.

[para]
Below is a sample configuration for two pools. The referenced Tcl
variables have to be set earlier to appropriate values.

[example_begin]
 ns_section ns/db/pool/pool1 {
   ns_param	Connections        15
   ns_param     LogMinDuration     10ms
   ns_param	LogSQLerrors       $debug
   ns_param	driver             postgres
   ns_param	DataSource         ${db_host}:${db_port}:${db_name}
   ns_param	user               $db_user
   ns_param	password           ""
 }
 ns_section ns/db/pool/pool2 {
   ns_param	Connections        5
   ns_param     LogMinDuration     10ms
   ns_param	LogSQLerrors       $debug
   ns_param	driver             postgres
   ns_param	DataSource         ${db_host}:${db_port}:${db_name}
   ns_param	user               $db_user
   ns_param	password           ""
 }
[example_end]


